# The `[[..]]` operator

`[[..]]` is HQL's **wiki-link operator**. Written around a name, it is an
expression that denotes what that name addresses in the current namespace:

```hql
alice = [[alice]]
```

Nothing wraps it. The brackets are the resolution: the source text that names a
target and the point at which the namespace is consulted are the same
characters, which is what makes the failure modes below attributable to a place
in the program.

The notation is the one an author already writes in a card body, so a cell and
its surrounding prose address the vault the same way. That is the operator's
strongest argument: HQL does not invent a second addressing syntax for the
documents it is embedded in.

## What it yields

The result type follows the **granularity of the address**, not the role of the
target:

```hql
[[alice]]           : Doc       // the document
[[alice#Bio]]       : Section   // a heading fragment
[[alice#^claim]]    : Block     // an anchored block
```

These are HyperMarkDown's three granularities; see [[doc-type]] for the body
structure they name.

What the operator does **not** yield is a [[card-type|Card]]. Card-ness is a
knowledge property that has to be established, not something a name can know
about its target, so the narrowing is a separate and fallible step:

```hql
doc  : Doc  = [[alice]]
card : Card = doc as Card        // a narrowing, and it can fail
```

The spelling of that narrowing remains unresolved.

## The brackets are not a call

Inside the brackets is a **name**, resolved by the vault's rules. It is not an
expression, not a string and not a path:

```hql
[[alice]]              // a name in this namespace
[[people/alice]]       // a name qualified by module
```

A consequence worth stating: the operator has no variable form. Addressing a
target whose name is computed at runtime is a different operation, over `Ref`
values, and it needs its own spelling. What that is, and whether a name can be
interpolated into the brackets at all, are open.

Display text is HMD's, not HQL's. `[[alice|Alice B.]]` addresses the same
document as `[[alice]]` and the operator returns the same value; the alternative
text is for the rendered body. Whether HQL source accepts the pipe form at all
is open, and there is an argument that it should not, since `|` is the pipe
operator — see [[pipes]].

## Resolution is contextual

The operator is not a constant. The same three words in two different cards can
denote two different documents, because a bare name is probed against named
imports, then the spine of ancestor modules, then wildcard import origins, then
autodiscovery, with ambiguity an error and no ranking rule to break it.

That machinery belongs to the vault, and so the operator does too: it has the
horizon a wikilink has, and never reaches into a neighbouring vault. See
[[card-type]] for the vault and namespace it needs, and [[hql/extensions]] for
where imports are declared.

## Failure

Resolution can fail in HMD's own ways: a target that does not exist, a fragment
missing from a document that does, a name matching several documents, or a
target escaping the namespace root. HMD treats an unresolved link as a *warning*
by design — writing forward to a card that has not been written yet is permitted,
and the diagnostic report is a to-do list. HQL must not turn that into a hard
error by accident.

An operator has to produce a value, which sharpens the question rather than
answering it. The candidates are an `Option`-typed result, a value that carries
its unresolved state, and a diagnostic that fails the program. Since absence is
`Option` and there is no null, an operator that yields `Doc` outright is the
reading hardest to defend. How failure is carried is the largest open question
about this operator.

## References at runtime

A name written in HQL source is this operator. A reference obtained from a value
is a different situation, and the two should not be conflated: a document's
links carry references that have not been resolved, and reading them is
ordinary.

```hql
doc.uplinks : Seq<Link>
```

A `Link` is structural connectivity, conceptually `Link(this, ref)`, and it does
not assert a semantic relation — see [[doc-type]] for uplinks and downlinks.
What turns such a reference into its target, and whether `Ref[T]` survives as a
type, are open.

## Links from a cell

An occurrence of `[[alice]]` in a card body is a link, and contributes an uplink
from that card and a downlink to `alice`. Whether the same occurrence *inside an
HQL cell* in that card does so as well is open. Arguing yes: the vault should be
able to see that the cell depends on `alice`, and a dependency invisible to the
graph is a dependency that breaks silently. Arguing no: a cell may address a
document it merely computes over, and manufacturing structural connectivity out
of a query would inflate the graph with edges no author wrote.

The question is not decidable from the notation, which is the same in both
places. It needs a decision about whether computed addressing is authorship.

See [[doc-type]] for what resolution yields, [[card-type]] for the vault that
resolves it, [[pipes]] for composition, and [[hql/design-status]] for what
remains undecided.
